# cp .env.example .env
# .env is gitignored (the repo root .gitignore's `.env` rule covers this path too).

# GitHub Personal Access Token.
#
#   classic       : the whole `repo` scope
#   fine-grained  : this repository only + Repository permissions ▸ Administration: Read and write
#                   (the runner-registration API requires the Administration permission)
#
# ★ Why a PAT rather than a single-use registration token (RUNNER_TOKEN): the runner re-registers itself when
#   the container is recreated or the token expires. A registration token dies within an hour.
#
# ⚠️ THIS TOKEN CAN ADMINISTER THE REPOSITORY. Give it a short expiry and rotate it.
ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A value identifying THIS machine. The runner's name becomes {{NAME_KEBAB}}-<this value>.
#
# ★ REQUIRED, and it MUST DIFFER PER MACHINE. The runner image calls config.sh with --replace, so if two
#   machines share a name THE ONE THAT STARTS LATER STEALS THE EARLIER ONE'S REGISTRATION. The dispossessed
#   machine still has /runner-data/.runner, cannot re-register, and falls into a 401 restart loop — silently
#   offline, while GitHub shows one apparently healthy runner.
#
# Use something that identifies the machine (a username or hostname, say).
RUNNER_HOST_ID=

# The label list this runner registers with. Empty → `docker`, which takes CI jobs but not deployments.
#
# ★ On THE ONE MACHINE designated as deploy host, widen it to:
#     RUNNER_LABELS=docker,deploy-host
#   deploy.yml's runs-on requires deploy-host. The deploy target configuration (SERVER_HOST, OAUTH_ISSUER_URI,
#   DB_HOST) is a single set of repo variables, so if any machine could take the deploy job it would deploy to
#   the wrong machine while the smoke test hits the intended host and went green.
#
# ⚠️ Labels are fixed AT REGISTRATION TIME. Editing this on an already-registered runner changes nothing —
#   attach the label under GitHub Settings ▸ Actions ▸ Runners instead.
RUNNER_LABELS=
